Date: Thu, 1 Apr 93 11:37:19 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: TECH: Entropic costs of computation

This post will be rather long -- I figured it was better to respond
to everything at once than make little snippets.  Plus, this way I can
pull out excerpts (hopefully not out of context!) to illustrate the
points I want to make.  Thanks to everybody who has participated so
far in this discussion.  I hope it can continue, because nothing at all
has been settled.

First, for the purposes of discussion, let's restrict our attention to
molecular rod-logic computers, because they are the subject of JoSH's
article in Extropy #10, they are the only design I've seen for which
the tiny supposed entropic costs mean anything, and they allow us to
dispense with considerations of electricity.  Now, there are two
distinct issues involved here:

1) Heat dissipation caused by the mechanism itself.  This is what Tom
   Litow seemed to be talking about when he wrote

> the heat produced is from the work done by the electric
> current through a resistive medium, and I would expect the
> total entropy of the computational system to thereby increase -
> "caused" by the electric current, _not_ any change in uncertainty
> in our knowledge of the register contents.

This is an interesting issue of its own, but Tim May points out:

> The effect Derek is referring to--and questioning--is much
> more basic and subtle than stuff about resistive heating of
> wires...it is basic to the nature of computation.

In particular, what I am questioning is the necessity of taking
special care to make the computation "reversible" -- by somehow
recording intermediate states of the computation.  In Tom's explanation,
such mechanisms would INCREASE the dissipation because there are more
rods banging into one another.  So that's not it.

Ray Cromwell's point is similar to Tom's in that it doesn't talk about
information per se.  He imagines a digital gate which takes three inputs
and produces one output and wonders where the extra energy goes.  However,
as he points out, this might not apply to rod logic and anyway this problem
has nothing to do with _inherent_ costs to computation, just to dissipation
caused by banging around electrons or rods.

2) Hal Finney introduces the subtler issue at hand:

> any write operation (whether a 0 or a 1) will cost energy
> because you have forgotten what value was held before.  It is the act
> of forgetting which causes the energy cost.  The operation is irreversible:
> 0->0  and  1->0  would be the state transitions for writing a zero.

See my response below to JoSH's article for my complaint about this.

> Now, theoretically, if you could deduce what had been there before even
> after you have written the zero (like, if it was an earlier result of
> the calculation which can be deduced from the current global state of
> your computer), then you haven't lost information and in principle the
> 0 could be stored without costing energy.

Here's where it gets tricky.  I will present my confusions by arguing
a contrary case; hopefully somebody will point out the flaws in my arguments.

1) It is ALWAYS "theoretically" possible to "deduce what had been there
   before" (time t - 1) in the case of deterministic computations -- simply
   restart the computation from its initial state and run it until time
   t - 1 and look!  That sounds stupid, but really isn't different from the
   "unwinding" involved in the cascade-thingie.

2) The physical laws that caused the rod-jostling in the first place
   are reversible -- NO MATTER WHAT, nothing has been "erased" -- simply
   reverse the velocities of the particles and you'll get back the
   old value!  This argument is the crux of my displeasure at the "macrostate"
   view of nanocomputers and demons, and I will expand on this below.

3) Who is the 'you' in Hal's "theoretically, if you could deduce what had
   been there before"  ???  An omniscient being, or an actual observer?
   This is critical.  If omniscient, see argument (2); if actual, then
   what is to be the time unit of our deduction?  I'll NEVER be able to
   deduce a preceding microstate from my observation, for ANY delta T in
   the past.  Do I have to pay a penalty for that in a continuous fashion?
   If not, who decides what time in the past corresponds to the penalty
   point?  Can I go all the way back to the beginning of the computation?

Now, a technical detail or two.  In Schneider's J Theor Biol article,
he deduces teh supposed dissipation minimum:

deltaS = -k log2(R)    [k is Boltzmann's constant]

  where deltaS is the change in entropy and R is the gain of information,
  and this equation "gives a direct, quantitative relationship between the
  decrease in entropy of a molecular machine and the information that it
  gains during an operation"  [I will ignore for now the wanton disregard
  for units in the derivation of this formula, but may come back to it
  later -- more obvious objections first].

  * what is an *operation*?  Is there an a priori basis for choosing how
  an operation is defined?  Suppose I define an operation as an entire,
  involved computation, running for a bazillion years -- the program is
  such that it leaves the memory of the machine in the same state in which
  it started except that the single output bit (it's a yes/no computation,
  for the purpose of example) gets set to the answer.  I've only gained
  one bit of information, regardless of whether there's special "reversibility"
  mechanisms inside the computer.  Right?

I will finish this by referring to JoSH's article in Extropy #10, where
he tries to make all this clear; however, he just confused me even more.
Apparently, the idea is supposed to be that if the machine is in a particular
"macrostate", and then we write a zero into some (1-bit) register, then the
number of microstates that could make up the new macrostate is smaller,
unless we dissipate some energy, since the "macrostate" of the machine after
the operation has a zero in that register, but the previous "macrostate"
could have (from the point of view of the later macrostate) had a one or
a zero in that register -- a macrostate with twice as many microstates as
the macrostate afterward.

But this is completely silly because of the arbitrary selection of what
we choose to call a "macrostate", and which parts of the machine we
include in that description.  JoSH says "This does sound a bit strange",
and then goes on to explain with Maxwell's Demon.  I didn't see how
that explained anything at all!  Whether or not the Demon records the
things it did, the resulting state can be reversed quite simply by reversing
the velocities of the particles:  that is, in reality there has been no
change in the volume of the phase space -- the only way it would appear
that way is with a particularly pernicious choice of "macrostate" -- and
I can ALWAYS choose macrostates in such a way that the same problem will
occur, by arbitrarily choosing to leave out as "unobserved" particular
aspects of the starting or final state.

Help!!!

derek
