Date: Thu, 1 Apr 93 11:10:50 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: HUMOR: UFO's From Future?
X-Reposting-Policy: Redistribute freely

I've been interested in UFO's since I was a boy.  Our public library
had lots of books about flying saucers, aliens, sightings, etc.  I
would read these at night and get a deliciously scary thrill imagining
aliens hiding in the shadows.

Today, though, I am totally skeptical about these issues.  I am an amateur
astronomer and a private pilot so I've spent a fair amount of time watching
the skies.  I've never seen a UFO or anything like one.  Some of the material
presented on TV as mysterious and unexplainable is transparently obvious.
One show depicted a mysterious light in the sky moving in crazy jiggles
and changing colors rapidly.  This was clearly a case of a hand-held camera
with poor "seeing" (turbulent air) leading to the color changes.  These kinds
of color flashes are common and pretty sights through the telescope.

Once I drove 200 miles to see the mysterious "ghost lights" of Marfa, TX.
I saw lights, but they looked to me to be due to atmospheric refraction.
I had hoped for something far more dramatic and was disappointed.

The wild stories of UFO abductions and alien attacks I have to assign
to fantasy.  The even wilder stories about the U.S. government working
with or warring against these aliens fall into the same category.  The world
would be a more interesting place, perhaps, if these stories were true.
But for me there are simpler and more concise explanations without invoking
supernatural alien powers.

Hal Finney
hal@alumni.caltech.edu

