Date: Thu, 01 Apr 93 20:36:53 +0100
From: Rich Walker <grlw1@cus.cam.ac.uk>
Subject: Traffic guide

I've pondered the matter of improving body transporting facilities a little,
and my personal two favourite methods are:
1) The old Batman movie where the world leaders (? Ambassadors?) get 
dehydrated, and later rehydrated. Given the rather good results obtained there
from mixing individuals, either people could be shipped in test-tube form, or
for moments where it was more important to get lots of people moved than to get
specific people moved (rush hours, football games, military manoeuvers, office
parties moving to the restaurant) just dehyrate them, and tip them all into a
bulk-tanker... The blending could do wonders for removing cultural and social
inhibitions... (;->, in case you didn't guess)
2) Proximity detection, hooked into the steering and engine. Not a nasty harsh
system, just something that made it harder to do silly things. So, the front 
detector is hooked up to the brakes, and the rear to the accelerator; the side
detectors go sirectly into the steering. Then, if you get too close to something
at the sides, the car tends to drift away from it. If you're too close to the c
car in front, you slow down. And so forth.
  This has one or two neat features: if properly calibrated, parking in tight
spaces could be done by pointing the car at it, and rolling forwards; the 
system manages (most of) the obstacle avoidance. Also, it _should_ serve to
eliminate one of the main causes of traffic jams: driver reaction time. If
ten cars are waiting at the lights, and the front one moves, it takes, IMHO,
2-4 seconds for each later driver to start moving. Cut this down to .2 to .4 
seconds, and you can get 10 times the number of cars through a green light...
  Okay, I'm sure someone is going to regard this as infringing on their
freedom as a driver; myself, I regard it as an incitement to exceedingly lazy
driving.
  (Incidentally, note that this system would provide almost-automatic car tr
trains; the only problem being to keep them together when the front one turns.
If they're on a major road with barriers centre and sides, then hopefully they
should be tending to avoid the barriers sufficiently to prevent the train
breaking down at a curve)

