Date: Thu, 1 Apr 93 11:38:30 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: TECH: Entropic costs of computation

From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
> 
>   Suppose I have a machine that executes the following operation:
>   Register A := Register A + Register B.  Now, supposedly this
>   sort of operation must pay a penalty for erasing the bits in
>   Register A.  But entropy as usually presented is a measure of
>   uncertainty, which would imply that the uncertainty about the
>   machine state has supposedly gone down (thus I must pay that
>   back somehow with heat or some other disordering process).  But
>   surely it hasn't!  I knew what the machine's state was before,
>   and I know it now.  If anything, I'm less sure because it could
>   have malfunctioned.  Put another way, there is no information
>   gain involved here, whether rubegoldbergian cascade-type mechanisms
>   are in place or not.  What gives?

I am not expert on this topic, but I will make a couple of points.
First, as it happens, Derek's specific example of A:=A+B is reversible.
At the end of that calculation it can be reversed by A:=A-B.  So
there is no inherent change in the information.

A simpler example is A:=0.  This is not reversible.  (There is nothing
magical about the '0'; any constant could be used.)  Now, paraphrasing
Derek's argument, I knew what A was before (it was whatever was there)
and I know what it is now (it is 0).  What is the change of information?

The difference is that the set of possible values available for A has
changed.  Previously A could have held anything.  Now it holds 0.  We
have therefore compressed the phase space and so we have gained information.
Think of A's previous value as being the result of observing some random
process and the phase space change becomes clearer.

(Now, if you say, what if A's value was _not_ random, but simply an
intermediate result in the calculation?  Then we have one specific and
reproducable value transforming into another and there is no phase
space change.  I discuss this below; in principle such a transformation
of A could be done with no cost, but in practice if A is physically
constructed such that it _could_ hold a random value and then be
cleared, then physical principles will require the clearing operation
to be costly.  So even if it happens that A didn't hold a random value
_this_time_, its construction will be such that there will be a heat
cost when we store the 0.)

> A tangential question:  It's been said that a coin that has been
> flipped must pay the penalty for one bit (heads/tails), but what
> if I'm interested instead in which of 1,000,000 evenly-spaced
> rotations that the coin ends up in?  Same coin, same toss, but
> now (because I have a different intent when watching it??!) it
> must pay the penalty for about ten bits.  Doesn't that seem sort
> of silly?

I think it would be more true to say that a coin which has been flipped
must pay the penalty for at least one bit.  More generally, any irreversible
physical process must pay the penalty for all the information that could
be derived from it.  The limitation on the information derivable will
be thermal, in my view.

Drexler's rod logic calculations assume only the one bit's worth of
information must be lost as heat.  Actually, if a rod could be measured
with more than one bit of precision then perhaps more than one bit's worth
must be lost, as I see it.  However, his rods are so tiny that thermal
fluctuations will prevent much more than one bit from being measured, so
perhaps his numbers are not too far off.  I seem to recall he was using
information considerations to produce lower bounds, anyway.
 
> Perhaps what's implied somehow is that MY uncertainty is reduced,
> and the physical measurement process required to perform that
> reduction is the cause of the balancing entropy.  But if this is
> so, surely it is only the RESULTS (state of output bits at some
> time) that must be measured, which would be independent of the
> number of computations used to produce them.

I think you're right that in principle you have to look at the different
entropies of the inputs and outputs of a calculation in order to derive
the minimum heat cost.  But in practice I'm not sure that a computer
could be constructed to only feel such a global constraint.  If one register
gets stored to I think there will be a heat cost, even if the information
is saved elsewhere in some form.  This is the price you pay for a modular
design; it is less efficient than the theoretical maximum.

The main sources I've used for learning about these topics are some papers
by Charles Bennett.  He also has had a couple of Scientific American
articles.

Hal Finney

