X-Reposting-Policy: Redistribute freely

Along the lines Rich suggested, I agree that a proximity detector system
would be useful.  I'd like to see sensors on all four sides of the car
which produce a map of the traffic situation surrounding the car.

I drive the freeways quite a bit, and I think it would be easy to design
a sensor to watch for the lane line and keep the car a certain distance
from it.  It would steer back and forth to keep you in the lane.  If
you combine this with a front proximity detector (radar or visual based),
it could slow you down if you approached a car.  It wouldn't be able to
change lanes, but you could think of it as a "super cruise control" for
easy driving.

You'd want to make sure the lane line wasn't going to disappear unexpectedly.
But this would be a much easier task for the road maintenance people to
undertake than adding new flashing lights or radio transmitters to the
road.  Just have them guarantee a lane line.  Maybe some change in the
lane pattern alerts the driver (via a beep from the lane follower) that
your lane is about to disappear.

The lane line detector could be as simple as three photodetectors aimed
at the road near the left front bumper - the middle one for a "correct"
distance, and inner and outer ones for corrections.  In practice you'd
want more for redundancy.

For this to work nicely, you'd first want "drive by wire" control of your
car.  I'm tired of steering wheels, accelerator and brake pedals.  I want
a joystick.  Push forward, speed up; pull back, slow down; side-to-side
means turn.  With a system like this adding computer control would be a
lot easier.

Autopilots are common on private airplanes.  They keep the wings level
and can keep you on course.  You still have to look out for traffic and
be ready in case the autopilot fails.  Similar advantages would come from
a car autopilot.

Most of these things could be done on a per-car basis without having to
change society (except a few adjustments to the liability laws ;-), and
they bring direct benefits to the owner.  At last you can read the paper
while you drive to work.

