From: Clark Reynard <clark@metal.psu.edu>
Subject: Speech for the defense

In response to Dale Worley:

I find your theory interesting; I do believe that in many cases what
is known as 'corporal punishment' is necessary, but that it has often
been over-used or mis-used entirely.  I believe that corporal punishment
is to be used only in cases in which it would prevent greater harm to
the child to avoid it.  For example, I believe--that even a "Spock" 
parent would agree that running across a busy intersection or drinking 
poison would be a behavior to be discouraged in the strongest possible
way.  No one but a fool would claim that it was best to allow a child
to kill itself out of ignorance.  

However, where the line is drawn is of utmost importance; for example,
beating a child on a refusal to go to bed might be considered cruel
if administered upon even a token resistance by the child.  However,
if the child then proceeded to throw a tantrum, destroy items, and
basically be 'intolerable to be around,' I, and many others, would 
believe that something other than logic was that for which the 
situation called.  However, behaviorists have shown--I am certain
that even the word 'behaviorist' is an obscenity in some circles--
that it is often quite enough merely to isolate the child in a neutral
environment until such time as the child will behave in an acceptable
manner.  Another option is the so-called 'token economy' in which
a preferred behavior is rewarded by a 'token,' either physical or,
in children old enough to comprehend abstractions, some form
of appreciation, to be represented in allowing the child some
activity or treat.  For example, NOT causing a scene (the 'bribe'
of which Worley spoke) would be worth a token, whereas some positive
behavior, such as making the dinner table without being asked to
do so, might be worth ten.  An ice cream cone might require a token,
whereas some desired activity (a movie and popcorn with a friend)
might be worth ten.  The only difficulty with this sort of system
is that often its benefit fades when the system is no longer implemented,
as it basically requires no moral lesson to be taught, but only
a practical lesson.

However, whether or not this instills discipline is not decided;
in one paragraph, Worley berates the selfish hippie generation,
not mentioning the fact that often this supposedly selfish group
was able to face physical violence in the name of an ideal; on
the other hand, he criticizes the lack of discipline of this generation,
which has paradoxically gone on from idealism to seventy-hour work
weeks for material objects.  The simple fact is that the hippie
generation was anything but selfish, and also anything but 
disciplined; whereis the yuppie generation it has become is
quite selfish, but certainly disciplined.  

Whether this might be related to the rearing of this generation is
yet to be determined; in college, often this generation failed 
miserably, as college brings no concrete, immediately-tangible
rewards; however, in a work environment, this generation has
been remarkably successful; it is worthwhile to note that today's
work environment closely resembles the 'token economy' in which
many of these children were raised.

Of course, this is a vast oversimplification, but may be valid
for proposing hypotheses.  Simply, I believe that Worley is both
right and wrong, but the ways in which he is wrong are, I find,
more interesting than the ways in which he is right.

